<body>

<main class="book-page">

  <h1>THE AWAKENED</h1>

  <h2>Book One: Before the Beginning</h2>

  <div class="chapter">
    Chapter One
  </div>

  <div class="chapter-title">
    The Garden Before Man
  </div>

  <p>
    The first light did not belong to the sun.
  </p>

  <p>
    There was no sun yet.
  </p>

  <p>
    The heavens were still young, and the earth was little
    more than a world of darkness, water, stone, and mist.
  </p>

  <p>
    But beneath that silence, something was changing.
  </p>

  <p>
    The waters began to move.
  </p>

  <p>
    At first they stirred almost imperceptibly, as though
    responding to a voice too deep for human ears.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then the waters separated.
  </p>

  <p>
    Land emerged.
  </p>

  <p>
    Mountains rose from the depths like the bones of an
    ancient giant.
  </p>

  <p>
    Valleys opened between them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Rivers began their journeys toward distant seas.
  </p>

  <p>
    And across the newborn earth, life appeared.
  </p>

  <p>
    Green.
  </p>

  <p>
    Wild.
  </p>

  <p>
    Untamed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Trees stretched their branches toward the heavens.
    Their roots reached deep into soil that had never known
    a footprint.
  </p>

  <p>
    Flowers opened beneath a sky that had never witnessed
    a human face.
  </p>

  <p>
    There were no walls.
  </p>

  <p>
    No borders.
  </p>

  <p>
    No weapons.
  </p>

  <p>
    No kings.
  </p>

  <p>
    There was nothing to conquer.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nothing to possess.
  </p>

  <p>
    Nothing to fear.
  </p>

  <p>
    The earth simply existed.
  </p>

  <div class="scene-break">
    ✦ ✦ ✦
  </div>

  <p>
    And somewhere beyond the visible heavens, the watchers
    observed.
  </p>

  <p>
    They had been given many names by those who would come
    much later.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some would call them messengers.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some would call them angels.
  </p>

  <p>
    Others would remember them as the Watchers.
  </p>

  <p>
    But at the beginning, they were simply beings who existed
    between the realm of the Creator and the world that was
    being formed below.
  </p>

  <p>
    They watched the mountains rise.
  </p>

  <p>
    They watched the rivers flow.
  </p>

  <p>
    They watched the first creatures move through the forests.
  </p>

  <p>
    And then they saw something that had never existed before.
  </p>

  <p>
    A place unlike any other.
  </p>

  <p>
    A garden.
  </p>

  <p>
    It was planted in the eastern lands, where four rivers
    would eventually divide the world known to humanity.
  </p>

  <p>
    The garden was not merely beautiful.
  </p>

  <p>
    It was alive.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every tree seemed to breathe.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every river reflected the light above it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The air carried the scent of flowers that had no names.
  </p>

  <p>
    And at the center of the garden stood two trees unlike
    the others.
  </p>

  <p>
    One was called the Tree of Life.
  </p>

  <p>
    The other was the Tree of the Knowledge of Good and Evil.
  </p>

  <p>
    They stood apart from the rest.
  </p>

  <p>
    Silent.
  </p>

  <p>
    Waiting.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Watchers looked upon them.
  </p>

  <p>
    And one among them spoke.
  </p>

  <p>
    <em>
      “Why give them a choice?”
    </em>
  </p>

  <p>
    Another answered:
  </p>

  <p>
    <em>
      “Because without choice, there can be no awakening.”
    </em>
  </p>

  <p>
    The first Watcher remained silent.
  </p>

  <p>
    Below them, the garden waited.
  </p>

  <p>
    But there was still no man.
  </p>

  <p>
    Not yet.
  </p>

  <div class="scene-break">
    ✦ ✦ ✦
  </div>

  <p>
    The Creator looked upon the earth.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then He reached toward the dust.
  </p>

  <p>
    And the dust began to move.
  </p>

  <p>
    It gathered.
  </p>

  <p>
    It shaped itself beneath an unseen hand.
  </p>

  <p>
    A form emerged.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two arms.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two legs.
  </p>

  <p>
    A chest.
  </p>

  <p>
    A face.
  </p>

  <p>
    Eyes that had never seen.
  </p>

  <p>
    A mouth that had never spoken.
  </p>

  <p>
    A heart that had never beaten.
  </p>

  <p>
    The figure lay motionless upon the earth.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Watchers watched.
  </p>

  <p>
    The garden became completely silent.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then the Creator leaned toward the form.
  </p>

  <p>
    And breathed.
  </p>

  <p>
    The first breath entered the man.
  </p>

  <p>
    His chest rose.
  </p>

  <p>
    His heart began to beat.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once.
  </p>

  <p>
    Twice.
  </p>

  <p>
    Again.
  </p>

  <p>
    His fingers moved.
  </p>

  <p>
    His eyes opened.
  </p>

  <p>
    And humanity entered the story.
  </p>

  <p>
    The man looked toward the heavens.
  </p>

  <p>
    He did not know what he was seeing.
  </p>

  <p>
    He did not know the names of the stars.
  </p>

  <p>
    He did not know the meaning of death.
  </p>

  <p>
    He did not know the difference between good and evil.
  </p>

  <p>
    He did not even know that he was alone.
  </p>

  <p>
    But somewhere within him was a memory older than his
    birth.
  </p>

  <p>
    A memory of the breath that had entered him.
  </p>

  <p>
    A memory he would carry even after he forgot almost
    everything else.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Creator looked upon him.
  </p>

  <p>
    And for the first time, the man heard a voice.
  </p>

  <p>
    <em>“Rise.”</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    The man stood.
  </p>

  <p>
    He looked across the garden.
  </p>

  <p>
    And the Creator gave him a name.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Adam.</strong>
  </p>

  <p>
    The first human name.
  </p>

  <p>
    The beginning of a story that would stretch across
    thousands of generations.
  </p>

  <p>
    Adam took his first step.
  </p>

  <p>
    The earth received his foot.
  </p>

  <p>
    The garden remained silent.
  </p>

  <p>
    But high above, one of the Watchers looked toward the
    Tree of Knowledge.
  </p>

  <p>
    And something in his expression changed.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because he understood something the man did not.
  </p>

  <p>
    The garden contained a choice.
  </p>

  <p>
    And one day, humanity would have to make it.
  </p>

